Rules of Thumb for void *

Use for generic buffers, allocators (malloc returns void *), and callbacks.
Cast to the actual type before arithmetic or dereference.
Keep size information alongside the pointer when the type is erased.
Do not cast void * to function pointers (different category in C).

Pointer Arithmetic (Brief)
Arithmetic scales by the size of the pointed-to type:
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main(void) {
    int arr[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
    int *p = arr;  /* same as &arr[0] */

    printf("*p       = %d\n", *p);       /* 10 */
    printf("*(p+1)   = %d\n", *(p + 1)); /* 20 */
    printf("p[2]     = %d\n", p[2]);     /* 30 — syntactic sugar */

    p++;
    printf("after p++: %d\n", *p);       /* 20 */
    return 0;
}
p[i] is defined as *(p + i).

